### Step 1: Calculate acceleration of the particle

Given electric field $\vec{E} = -400 \sqrt{3}\hat{y}\ \text{NC}^{-1}$. The acceleration of the charged particle (mass m, charge q) in the y-direction is given by $a_y = \frac{qE_y}{m}$.
$a_y = \left(\frac{q}{m}\right) E_y$ = ($10^{10}$\ $\text{Ckg}^{-1}$) \times (-400 $\sqrt{3}$\ $\text{NC}^{-1}$)
$a_y$ = -400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$\ $\text{ms}^{-2}$
The magnitude of acceleration is $|a_y| = 400 \sqrt{3} \times 10^{10}\ \text{ms}^{-2}$.

### Step 2: Determine projection angles using range formula

The initial speed of the particle is $u = 2 \sqrt{10} \times 10^6\ \text{ms}^{-1}$. The horizontal distance to the target T is $R = 5\ \text{m}$. For projectile motion under constant vertical acceleration, the range is given by $R = \frac{u^2 \sin 2\theta}{|a_y|}$.
5 = $\frac${(2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$)^2 $\sin$ 2$\theta$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
5 = $\frac${(4 \times 10 \times $10^{12}$) $\sin$ 2$\theta$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
5 = $\frac${40 \times $10^{12} \sin$ 2$\theta$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
$\sin$ 2$\theta = \frac${5 \times 400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}{40 \times $10^{12}$} = $\frac${2000 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}{40 \times $10^{12}$}
$\sin$ 2$\theta = \frac${50 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}{$10^{12}$} = $\frac${50 $\sqrt{3}$}{100} = $\frac${$\sqrt{3}$}{2}
Therefore, $2\theta = 60^\circ$ or $2\theta = 120^\circ$.
$\theta$ = 30^$\circ$\ $\text{ or }$\ $\theta$ = 60^$\circ$
This confirms option (B).

### Step 3: Calculate time of flight for each angle

The time of flight for projectile motion under constant vertical acceleration is given by $T = \frac{2u \sin \theta}{|a_y|}$.
For $\theta = 30^\circ$:
$T_1 = \frac${2 \times (2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$) \times $\sin$ 30^$\circ$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
$T_1 = \frac${2 \times (2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$) \times $\frac{1}{2}$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$} = $\frac${2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
$T_1 = \frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{200 $\sqrt{3}$} \times $10^{-4} = \frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{200 $\sqrt{3}$} \times $\frac{100}{100}$ \times $10^{-4} = \frac${100 $\sqrt{10}$}{200 $\sqrt{3}$} \times $10^{-6} T_1 = \frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{2 $\sqrt{3}$} \times $10^{-6} = \sqrt${$\frac{10}{4 \times 3}$} \times $10^{-6} = \sqrt${$\frac{10}{12}$} \times $10^{-6} = \sqrt${$\frac{5}{6}$}\ $\mu \text{s}$
For $\theta = 60^\circ$:
$T_2 = \frac${2 \times (2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$) \times $\sin$ 60^$\circ$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
$T_2 = \frac${2 \times (2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$) \times $\frac${$\sqrt{3}$}{2}}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$} = $\frac${2 $\sqrt{10} \sqrt{3}$ \times $10^6$}{400 $\sqrt{3}$ \times $10^{10}$}
$T_2 = \frac${2 $\sqrt{10}$ \times $10^6$}{400 \times $10^{10}$} = $\frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{200} \times $10^{-4} T_2 = \frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{200} \times $\frac{100}{100}$ \times $10^{-4} = \frac${100 $\sqrt{10}$}{200} \times $10^{-6} T_2 = \frac${$\sqrt{10}$}{2} \times $10^{-6} = \sqrt${$\frac{10}{4}$} \times $10^{-6} = \sqrt${$\frac{5}{2}$}\ $\mu \text{s}$
This confirms option (C).
The final answer is (B, C).
